omm turning it  into a business but they both helped get  me there I didn't commit to doing either  of them full time I was dipping my toes  in offering some expertise learning  along the way and seeing if it was a  right fit for me or not and the only way  for me to figure that out was to get in  and start trying to do it keeping a  journal help so I could figure out what  I enjoy doing and what I didn't enjoy  doing one of the lightbulb turning  points for me was when I started doing  presentations about entrepreneurship and  I realized I wanted to do more of these  I loved doing them so much I loved  helping entrepreneurs and I had to keep  finding a way to do it to do it and  you'll get to that moment and it's a  process of self-discovery that you have  to go to you're on your journey but you  don't start a journey just sitting at  home you know you've got to go out there  and start trying different things that  sound appealing but not with a huge  expectation that this is gonna turn into  your your million dollar idea if you put  massive expectations on things then  you're setting yourself up for failure  go out experience it see if you like  doing it so if you want to do it again  and again and again see if this is what  you were born to do or not you've got to  find that and you will if you look at  Steve Jobs founder of Apple he was in  college universe and he dropped out he  dropped out of his classes because he  wasn't enjoying it and he started  dropping in on classes that talked about  typography and fonts and he had no idea  what that would mean for him he just  found that interesting and he dropped in  on computer building clubs  and again he had no idea what it was  gonna mean he was just following  something that was curious to him and  led to the creation of Apple and this  huge company and massive wealth for him  but at the time was scary you know and  his friends and family saying you're  dropping out of university you're  dropping out of college 